  <subsection xml:id="sec-course-info">
    <title>Course Information</title>

    <p>
      This is the syllabus for Analysis of Differential Equations <term> (MTH 304-01)</term> 
      for Fall 2026. It is a 3-credit course.
      <!-- use a description list (dl) for short items -->
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Instructor</title>

          <p>
            Prof. Firas Hindeleh, MAK A-2-116, <phone>(616) 331-3739</phone>, 
            <url href="mailto:hindelef@gvsu.edu">hindelef@gvsu.edu</url>.
                      </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Student Hours\ availability </title>

          <p>
            I will be holding my office hours on TR 10-10:50AM at my office and on Mondays 10-10:50 at the Peer Collaboration Space PCS (MAK A-2-610). Outside office hours, feel free to stop by my office. Please note that I typically only check email and other messages between 8am and 5pm on weekdays. If you send a message that needs a response during those times, you can expect to get a response within 6 hours. 
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Class meets</title>

          <p>
            MWF 9-9:50AM MAK B1-110 .
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Course Description</title>

          <p>
            Solution methods for first order and second order linear equations (including power series and numerical methods). The linear algebra of linear systems and their solutions. Qualitative analysis of linear and nonlinear systems: phase plane; existence and uniqueness; stability, and applications in physical, biological, and social sciences.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Prerequisite</title>

          <p>
            MTH 203 and MTH 204.</p>
        </li>

         <li>
          <title>Textbook (Optional for reference) </title>

          <p>
            <pubtitle>Differential Equations</pubtitle> by Blanchard, Devaney, and Hall, 4th edition. 
This is an older edition. The PDF source file is available online, or you can purchase a super cheap used copy online.
          </p>
          <p>
            (Optional Reference) Elementary Differential Equations with Boundary Value Problems, William Trench, online at <url href="https://digitalcommons.trinity.edu/mono/8/"> https://digitalcommons.trinity.edu/mono/8/</url>
          </p>

          <p>
            (Optional Reference) Ordinary Differential Equations, Gabriel Nagy, online at <url href="https://users.math.msu.edu/users/gnagy/teaching/ode.pdf"> https://users.math.msu.edu/users/gnagy/teaching/ode.pdf/ </url>
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
    <!-- Use paragraphs to break apart subsections -->
    <paragraphs>
      <title>Topics</title>

      <p>
        
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>First order differential equations (2-3 Weeks) </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>Systems of linear differential equations (4-5 Weeks).</p>
          </li>

           <li>
            <p>Inhomogeneous systems of linear differential equations (4-5 Weeks).) </p>
          </li>

           <li>
            <p>Nonlinear systems of differential equations (2-3 Weeks)  </p>
          </li>

        </ul>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
     <paragraphs>
      <title>Course Objectives</title>
      <p>After successful completion of the course, students will be able to… 
        <ol marker="1.">
          <li>
          Analyze first order differential equations using slope fields.
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
         Solve certain first order differential equations analytically.
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
          Analyze autonomous systems of linear first order differential equations using directions fields and linear algebra. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
          Analyze higher-order linear differential equations (or a linear system of differential equations) with constant coefficients (both homogeneous and non-homogeneous) using linear algebra. 
          </li>
        

        
          <li>
           Qualitatively analyze a first order nonlinear autonomous system of differential equations. 
          </li>
        </ol>
        </p>
         </paragraphs>
</subsection>
